  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM EST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nA 996mb low swirls over the UP this morning (bringing much needed \nrain to a drought-striken area), with a secondary shortwave trough \naloft digging into extreme eastern Iowa. This has spawned a fairly \nwidespread area of rain and drizzle upstream. This feature is \nstill digging south-southeast this hour but is expected to lift \nnortheast this afternoon. As it does so, scattered showers will \nmove through for the afternoon. Remaining somewhat breezy once \nmore with a modest pressure gradient still in play. Temperatures\nonly in the 50s today with only modest cold air advection noted\naloft due to boundary- parallel flow. However, a strong push of\nCAA rushes in this evening/tonight as the primary trough axis \n(digging in through Minnesota this morning) sweeps through. This\nCAA and lift promotes widespread showers followed by lake \neffect rain showers. If you're a snow lover, this is truly a \nwaste of what would otherwise be an impressive lake effect snow \nset up. High resolution CAMS suggest QPF through the event \nof 0.30-0.50\". Equilibrium levels crest at nearly 9,000 ft, \nindicative of excellent lake induced lift. A single band of \nrain is anticipated late Thursday night through Friday night \nthat follows the eastern shore and wavers over La Porte and \nwestern St. Joseph (IN) counties. Lake effect comes to and end \nSaturday morning as equilibrium levels fall in response to \nrising heights aloft with the parent trough moving off. \n\nCompared to recent temperatures, very cold early Sunday morning in \nthe wake of this system as skies clear and high pressure descends \nover the Great Lakes. Lows will be near freezing which is just shy \nof normal: 35 degrees. Over the course of next week, a classic sine \nwave pattern takes shape at 500mb with a towering Central US\nridge bookended by troughs. We'll be on the edge of seasonably \ncool northwest flow. If northwest flow wins out, we could see \nepisodes of light rain (though Pacific moisture transport is \npoor). Alternatively, mild and dry conditions could prevail if \nthe ridge to our west shifts east late in the week.",
